🌐 Web query:

`Bun ffi Buffer lifetime keep alive ptr(Buffer) garbage collection`

💡 Result:

In Bun’s FFI, the object you create in JS (a Buffer/ArrayBuffer/TypedArray returned by bun:ffi helpers like toBuffer/toArrayBuffer) is what keeps the backing memory alive, not the raw pointer. If you only keep the numeric pointer (ptr(Buffer)) and drop the JS wrapper object, GC can run and free the underlying JS ArrayBuffer that bun:ffi is referencing; the pointer may then refer to freed memory. Key points from Bun docs: 1) bun:ffi does not manage native memory; you must free memory yourself. 2) If you convert a pointer to a JS view via toArrayBuffer/toBuffer and want Bun to coordinate cleanup with GC, you can pass a deallocator callback (and optional context). Bun will call that cleanup “once the garbage collector frees the underlying ArrayBuffer JavaScript object.” [1] So, for “keep alive ptr(Buffer) garbage collection”: - Keeping only ptr (a number/bigint address) does not inherently keep the underlying JS Buffer/ArrayBuffer alive. - To keep the memory/view alive, you must keep a live reference to the JS object (Buffer/TypedArray/DataView/ArrayBuffer) that bun:ffi created (or keep another JS object that retains it). - If you’re bridging memory whose lifetime you want tied to JS GC, use the deallocator mechanism in toArrayBuffer/toBuffer so that the cleanup happens when GC frees the JS backing ArrayBuffer [1]. This is the “official” lifetime/keep-alive pattern in Bun FFI. Example pattern (tie cleanup to GC): - Convert pointer to ArrayBuffer/Buffer with a deallocator callback/context so Bun calls your cleanup after GC frees the JS backing ArrayBuffer [1].

Citations:

- 1: https://bun.com/docs/runtime/ffi

