In Node.js, spawnSync returns a result object with these meanings: 1) result.status - Exit code of the subprocess, or null if the subprocess terminated due to a signal [1][2]. 2) result.signal - The signal used to kill the subprocess, or null if it did not terminate due to a signal [1][2]. So the combined behavior is: - If the child exits normally with exit code N: status = N, signal = null. - If the child is terminated by a signal (e.g., SIGKILL/SIGTERM): status = null, signal = that signal name [1][2]. 3) result.error - error is present (non-null) when the child process “failed or timed out”; and spawnSync will throw an Error that includes the full underlying spawnSync result if the process times out or has a non-zero exit code [1][2]. - For example, if the executable cannot be spawned (ENOENT), spawnSync returns an object with error set, and status/signal are null (and typically output/stdout/stderr are also null) [3]. 4) result.error.signal (if you’re looking at the Error object) - Node’s docs also describe error.signal as the signal that terminated the process [1][2]. Practical checks you can use: - If result.status === null: - the process likely died from a signal; check result.signal (and/or result.error.signal) [1][2]. - If result.error is non-null: - the spawn failed (e.g., ENOENT) or a timeout occurred; in these cases status will typically be null and stdout/stderr/output may be null as well [3].

Citations:

- 1: https://beta.docs.nodejs.org/child_process.html
- 2: https://nodejs.org/api/child_process.html
- 3: https://github.com/nodejs/node/issues/33458

